<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Calenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1432 CE – 1692 CE · capital Hanover</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Guelph principality between the Deister and the Leine that grew into the Electorate of Hanover and the dynasty of Britain's Georgian kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> lands of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg">Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg</a> were repeatedly divided. In 1432 the territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deister">Deister</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a>, named after Calenberg castle, became a separate principality under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-victorious">William the Victorious</a>, split off from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Calenberg was ruled from Calenberg castle and other residences, and from 1636 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. Duchess Elisabeth, regent in the 1540s, introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. The principality absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> in 1495 and later Grubenhagen. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-frederick-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">John Frederick</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> to Hanover as librarian in 1676, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover">Ernest Augustus</a> built a more centralised state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1692 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Leopold</a> I made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover">Ernest Augustus</a> an elector, and Calenberg became the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a>, which absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-luneburg">Principality of Lüneburg</a> in 1705. His son George became King of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1714.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-calenberg.jpg" alt="Principality of Calenberg" width="320" height="111"><figcaption>Image: Martin Zeiller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_und_Ampt_Calenberg_by_Caspar_Merian_1654.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1432 CE – 1692 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanover</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elisabeth of Brandenburg, John Frederick, Ernest Augustus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brunswick-Calenberg, Calenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=principality-of-calenberg" aria-label="Principality of Calenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 370 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Calenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 626L5225 650M5219 626L5237 632M5219 626L5229 694M5219 626L5219 626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 650h0M5237 632h0M5229 694h0M5219 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hanover. <a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=principality-of-calenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
